add('1:a-i',['A3.1.1'],'EMPLOYEE(EmployeeID,Name,RoleID) uses EmployeeID to identify each row uniquely. State its primary key.','EmployeeID.',[(1,'EmployeeID.')],[4])
add('1:a-ii',['A3.1.1'],'EMPLOYEE(EmployeeID PK,Name,RoleID FK) refers to ROLE(RoleID PK,Grade). State the foreign key in EMPLOYEE.','RoleID.',[(1,'RoleID.')],[4])
add('1:b',['A3.1.1'],'Each employee holds one role; a role can be held by many employees. Employees can work in many branches and each branch has many employees. Describe the ROLE-to-EMPLOYEE and EMPLOYEE-to-BRANCH relationships.','ROLE to EMPLOYEE is one-to-many. EMPLOYEE to BRANCH is many-to-many, normally represented through a link table.',[(1,'Role to employee one-to-many.'),(1,'Employee to branch many-to-many.')],[4],status='B',source_issues=['Source stem permits staff in several departments but STAFF has only one DepartmentID; MS accepts one-to-many or many-to-many. Adaptation states intended cardinalities explicitly.'])
add('1:c',['A3.3.2'],'Outline how a database query could find employees in a specified pay grade rather than showing every employee record.','It selects the required fields and applies a condition on the pay grade, returning only matching employee rows.',[(1,'Selects required data/fields.'),(1,'Filters rows using a condition.')],[4])
c="SELECT e.FirstName,e.Surname,r.Grade FROM Employee e JOIN Role r ON e.RoleID=r.RoleID WHERE e.Surname='Chen' AND r.Grade=8;"
add('1:d',['A3.3.2'],'EMPLOYEE(EmployeeID,FirstName,Surname,RoleID) and ROLE(RoleID,Grade) are related by RoleID. Construct SQL showing only FirstName,Surname and Grade for employees named Chen in grade8.','One solution:\n'+c,[(1,'Selects exactly three required fields.'),(1,'Uses both tables.'),(1,'Correct RoleID join.'),(1,'Both surname and grade filters with AND.')],[5],status='B',test_code='''import sqlite3
c=sqlite3.connect(':memory:');c.executescript("CREATE TABLE Employee(EmployeeID,FirstName,Surname,RoleID);CREATE TABLE Role(RoleID,Grade);INSERT INTO Role VALUES(1,8),(2,7);INSERT INTO Employee VALUES(1,'A','Chen',1),(2,'B','Chen',2),(3,'C','Mora',1);")
assert c.execute("SELECT e.FirstName,e.Surname,r.Grade FROM Employee e JOIN Role r ON e.RoleID=r.RoleID WHERE e.Surname='Chen' AND r.Grade=8").fetchall()==[('A','Chen',8)]''',check='SQLite join/filter executed on matching and nonmatching rows.',ms_summary='Original selects STAFF.FirstName,Surname and ROLE.PayGrade,joins RoleID,filters Waters and17.',source_issues=['Source MS first SQL example inconsistently uses ROLE/Roles; adapted query uses consistent names.'])
add('1:e',['A3.2.3'],'Outline why an integer type is appropriate for a field storing a whole-number pay grade used in numeric sorting.','Grades are whole numbers, so integers represent the allowed values without fractional parts. Numeric ordering places grade10 after9 instead of a character-based order such as10 before2.',[(1,'Whole-number values fit integer.'),(1,'Appropriate numeric sorting/calculation explanation.')],[5])
add('1:f',['A3.1.1','A3.3.5'],'Explain two ways to restrict disclosure of employee salary data in a relational database. Develop each in the context of team managers and payroll staff.','Use role-based access so payroll staff can access salary records while other users lack those permissions; this limits unnecessary exposure. Provide managers a restricted view containing only their team’s permitted columns and rows, hiding bank details and other teams’ salaries while allowing their defined task.',[(1,'Appropriate access permissions.'),(1,'Explains restriction to authorized payroll role.'),(1,'Concrete reduced salary exposure.'),(1,'Restricted view.'),(1,'Explains row/column subset for managers.'),(1,'Concrete protected bank/other-team data.')],[6],status='B',alternatives=['Accept a distinct developed encryption safeguard with correct key/access reasoning; do not count the same permissions point twice.'])
add('2:a',['A3.2.1'],'Describe the difference between conceptual and logical database schemas.','A conceptual schema gives a high-level view of entities and relationships. A logical schema specifies their structure in more detail, including attributes and keys, without choosing physical storage implementation.',[(1,'High-level conceptual entities/relationships.'),(1,'More detailed logical attributes/keys/structure.')],[7])
add('2:b',['A3.3.1'],'Explain why DDL is needed to implement an agreed database model, giving one suitable command.','DDL defines the schema in the DBMS. CREATE TABLE can turn a modelled entity and attributes into a table with columns and key constraints.',[(1,'Defines/implements schema structure.'),(1,'Correct command with structural example.')],[7])
add('2:c',['A3.2.1','A3.2.4'],'Explain two reasons to model an equipment-loan database before creating its tables.','Identifying entities and attributes checks that member, equipment and loan data support the required service instead of omitting essential facts. Defining keys and relationships lets each loan identify the correct member/item, supporting valid joins and avoiding ambiguous duplicated names.',[(1,'Identifies necessary entities/attributes.'),(1,'Explains support for intended use/avoided omission.'),(1,'Defines keys/relationships.'),(1,'Explains reliable linking/retrieval.')],[7],status='B')
add('2:d',['B2.3.2'],'A program accepts a day number only if1<=day<=31. Explain why passing that test does not prove that the entered day equals the value on an original form, and describe an additional check.','The range test rejects impossible numbers but accepts a mistyped value such as12 instead of21. Compare the entered value with the original form or use independently repeated entry and resolve differences. Rule conformity and faithful transcription are different checks.',[(1,'Range test checks permitted values,not intended source value.'),(1,'Concrete in-range transcription error.'),(1,'Appropriate source comparison/double-entry check.')],[8],status='B',adapt='Retains validation-versus-verification reasoning in a supplied programming selection context; no broad database-validation taxonomy is claimed.')
add('2:e',['A3.3.6'],'Outline how a transaction can avoid leaving only half of an account transfer in a database when an update fails.','Treat the debit and credit as one transaction. Commit both if successful; otherwise roll back the uncommitted changes so neither half remains.',[(1,'Both updates form one all-or-nothing transaction.'),(1,'Commit both or roll back failed partial updates.')],[8])
add('2:f',['A3.1.1'],'Outline how primary and foreign keys help keep loan/member data consistent.','A member primary key uniquely identifies each member. A loan’s foreign key must match a member key, preventing a loan from referring to a nonexistent member.',[(1,'Primary key uniqueness/correct cross-table link.'),(1,'Foreign-key rule prevents invalid references.')],[8])
add('3:a-i',['A3.1.1'],'Outline why computing an employee’s current age from stored date of birth can avoid an inconsistency caused by separately storing age.','A stored age becomes outdated at a birthday even when the date of birth is correct. Calculating it when needed avoids maintaining two dependent values that can disagree.',[(1,'Stored age can become outdated/inconsistent.'),(1,'Calculation avoids redundant dependent values/update need.')],[9],status='B',adapt='Retains derived-data reasoning as explicit redundancy/consistency benefit; does not claim a separate derived-field taxonomy.')
add('3:a-ii',['B2.3.1','B2.3.2'],'Describe how to calculate a person’s completed years of age from birth year/month/day and today’s year/month/day, assuming a valid ordinary date.','Subtract birth year from current year, then subtract one more if the current month/day is before the birthday this year.',[(1,'Year difference.'),(1,'Adjusts by one before this year’s birthday.')],[9],status='B',source_issues=['Some source MS formulas subtract years only and overstate age before the birthday. Adaptation explicitly calculates completed years.'])
add('3:b',['A3.2.5'],'Outline the difference between1NF and2NF.','1NF requires atomic field values and no repeating groups. 2NF additionally requires every non-key attribute to depend on the whole candidate key, removing partial-key dependencies.',[(1,'Correct1NF atomic/no-repeating-group requirement.'),(1,'2NF includes1NF and removes partial-key dependencies.')],[9],source_issues=['MS says2NF only applies to tables with composite keys; more precisely partial dependency concerns composite keys,while single-key1NF relations satisfy2NF automatically.'])
add('3:c',['A3.2.6'],'A booking system stores MemberID,MemberName,GuideID,GuideName,DepartmentID,DepartmentName,BookingID,StartTime. Each guide belongs to one department. MemberID determines MemberName; GuideID determines GuideName and DepartmentID; DepartmentID determines DepartmentName. Each unique BookingID refers to one member and guide at StartTime. Construct a3NF schema with keys.','MEMBER(MemberID PK,MemberName); DEPARTMENT(DepartmentID PK,DepartmentName); GUIDE(GuideID PK,GuideName,DepartmentID FK); BOOKING(BookingID PK,MemberID FK,GuideID FK,StartTime).',[(1,'MEMBER correct.'),(1,'DEPARTMENT correct.'),(1,'GUIDE attributes and primary key.'),(1,'DepartmentID foreign key in GUIDE.'),(1,'BOOKING key and StartTime.'),(1,'MemberID/GuideID foreign keys and no redundant names in BOOKING.')],[10],status='B',adapt='Supplies explicit dependencies and booking identity rather than inferring functional dependencies from a UI screenshot.')
